.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt INPUT 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm input
.Nd the event devices of the keyboards and buttons
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/input.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/input/event0
to
.Pa /dev/input/event31
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each input device the kernel drives is a node of
.Pa /dev/input ,
with Linux's evdev interface: major 13, minor 64 and up.
A node is listed only while its device is there, and a device takes the
lowest free node when it is found.
The PS/2 keyboard is
.Dq AT Translated Set 2 keyboard
and each USB keyboard
.Dq USB keyboard N ,
N being its place in the USB stack's table, as
.Dv EVIOCGNAME
gives them.
A keyboard's keys reach its node beside the console, which every keyboard
shares; see
.Xr console 4 .
.Pp
The ACPI devices of
.Xr acpi 4
are nodes too:
.Dq Power Button ,
.Dq Sleep Button
and
.Dq Video Bus ,
on the host's bus,
.Dv BUS_HOST .
Each reports a key pressed and then released at once, in two packets, with
no
.Dv MSC_SCAN ,
since the firmware's event has no key code of its own.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
gives whole
.Vt struct input_event
records, as many as fit in its count: the time of the event, its type, its
code and its value.
The time is two
.Vt long Ns s ,
the seconds and microseconds of the real time, so a record is 16 bytes on
i686 and 24 on x86-64, as
.In linux/input.h
lays it out under the C library's 64-bit
.Vt time_t .
.Pp
A key is reported as one packet of three records:
.Dv EV_MSC
.Dv MSC_SCAN
with the code the keyboard sent,
.Dv EV_KEY
with Linux's key code,
.Dv KEY_A
to
.Dv KEY_MAX ,
and the value 1 for a press and 0 for a release, and
.Dv EV_SYN
.Dv SYN_REPORT ,
which ends the packet.
The PS/2 keyboard's
.Dv MSC_SCAN
is its set-1 code with the release bit clear and 0x80 set after an E0
prefix, as Linux's keyboard driver gives a translated keyboard's: 0x1e for
A, 0xb0 for Volume Up.
A USB keyboard's is the key's usage in the keyboard page, 0x70004 for A.
A key with no key code gives the
.Dv MSC_SCAN
alone.
.Pp
The keyboards repeat in software, every keyboard alike: the last key
pressed that is not a modifier or a lock repeats 250 ms after its press and
then every 33 ms, Linux's times, until it is released.
A repeat is a packet of two records,
.Dv EV_KEY
with the value 2 and
.Dv EV_SYN
.Dv SYN_REPORT .
A press of a key that is down already, which a PS/2 keyboard sends while
the key is held, is dropped.
When the repeat's times change, each keyboard reports the ones that
changed as
.Dv EV_REP
.Dv REP_DELAY
and
.Dv REP_PERIOD ,
in milliseconds; when the console's LEDs change, each keyboard reports
those that changed as
.Dv EV_LED
.Dv LED_NUML ,
.Dv LED_CAPSL
and
.Dv LED_SCROLLL ,
1 for lit; each change ends with
.Dv SYN_REPORT .
.Pp
Each open has a queue of its own, of 64 records.
A read waits until a whole packet is queued and then returns as many whole
records as its buffer holds, so a buffer smaller than the packet splits it
and the next read returns the rest, as on Linux.
A reader that falls so far behind that its queue fills loses what it had:
the queue keeps an
.Dv EV_SYN
.Dv SYN_DROPPED
and the newest record, and the reader should drop what it reads up to the
next
.Dv SYN_REPORT
and ask for the keys held with
.Dv EVIOCGKEY .
A read waits for a packet unless the descriptor is nonblocking;
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN
while one is queued, and
.Dv POLLHUP
once the device has gone.
.Pp
The ioctls are Linux's:
.Bl -tag -width EVIOCGBIT
.It Dv EVIOCGVERSION
The interface's version, 0x010001.
.It Dv EVIOCGID
A
.Vt struct input_id :
the bus, 0x11 for the PS/2 controller and 0x03 for USB, the vendor, the
product and the version, 0.
.It Dv EVIOCGNAME , EVIOCGPHYS
The device's name and its place,
.Dq isa0060/serio0/input0
for the PS/2 keyboard and
.Dq usb-N/input0
for a USB keyboard, cut to the length the request gives, as Linux cuts
them; the answer is the bytes copied.
.It Dv EVIOCGUNIQ
.Er ENOENT :
no device has a serial number.
.It Dv EVIOCGBIT
The bits of the event types the device reports,
.Dv EV_SYN ,
.Dv EV_KEY
and, for a keyboard,
.Dv EV_MSC ,
.Dv EV_LED
and
.Dv EV_REP ;
of the keys it can report; of its
.Dv EV_MSC
codes,
.Dv MSC_SCAN ;
and of a keyboard's LEDs,
.Dv LED_NUML ,
.Dv LED_CAPSL
and
.Dv LED_SCROLLL .
The other types answer no bits, and
.Dv EV_REP
answers
.Er EINVAL ,
as on Linux.
.It Dv EVIOCGKEY
The bits of the keys down now.
.It Dv EVIOCGLED
The bits of a keyboard's LEDs lit; none for another device.
.It Dv EVIOCGPROP , EVIOCGSND , EVIOCGSW
No bits: no device here has properties, sounds or switches.
.It Dv EVIOCGRAB
With a nonzero argument, take the device for this open alone, so that no
other open reads its events until it gives it back, with 0, or closes.
The console still reads the keys.
.It Dv EVIOCGREP , EVIOCSREP
Read into, or set from, two unsigned ints a keyboard's repeat: the delay
before a held key's first repeat and the period between repeats, in
milliseconds.
Every keyboard shares one repeat, which the console's
.Dv KDKBDREP
also sets; see
.Xr console 4 .
A time above
.Dv INT_MAX
is left as it is, and a period of 0 turns repeat off.
A device that is no keyboard answers
.Er ENOSYS .
.El
.Pp
The nodes are root's, mode 0600, as Linux's are.
BusyBox's
.Cm acpid
reads them; see
.Xr busybox-util-linux 1 .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking read with no packet queued.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
.Dv EVIOCGRAB
of a device another open holds.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A read smaller than one record;
.Dv EVIOCGRAB
with 0 by an open that does not hold the device; or
.Dv EVIOCGBIT
of a type Linux does not answer.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
An open of a node with no device, or any call on an open whose device has
gone, such as a USB keyboard unplugged.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
.Dv EVIOCGREP
or
.Dv EVIOCSREP
on a device that is no keyboard.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another ioctl.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-util-linux 1 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr mixer 4
